  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\n- $f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$\n- $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n- $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={x}^{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is a surjection. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ choose $x={log}_{3}\u2061y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({log}_{3}\u2061y)={3}^{{log}_{3}\u2061y}=y.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{2}=2$ is $x=\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- III is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={x}^{2}=-1.$  In fact, ${x}^{2}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
